# --- Live server state: MUST NOT be tracked -------------------------------
# These files are written on the publishing server as videos go out, so they are
# the authoritative record of what has been published and of the day's API
# spend. Tracking them means ANY plain `git reset --hard` silently rewinds them:
# the automation then re-uploads live videos, and the budget guard under-counts.
# That is not hypothetical - it happened twice during this work, the second time
# from a bare `git reset --hard` that bypassed deploy/pull.sh entirely.
# Untracked, no reset can touch them at all.
#
# discovery_pool.json and analytics_history.json stay TRACKED on purpose: they
# are produced by the GitHub Actions analytics job, which is the freshest source.
posted_shorts.json
longform_out/posted_long.json
discovery_quota.json
trend_pool.json
yt_budget.json
shorts_generated/
topics_generated/

# --- Merged repost pipeline (project "yyy" / shortsdl) ----------------------
# repost_posted.json is the "already published" record for downloaded Shorts -
# same rule as posted_shorts.json: untracked, because a reset must never rewind
# it (that would re-upload the same video to the channel).
repost_posted.json
# Downloads, logs, indexes and the yt-dlp archive are per-server state.
repost_out/

# --- Per-run render artifacts: MUST NOT be tracked -------------------------
# These are produced during a run and deleted by cleanup_artifacts.py after a
# successful upload. Tracking them defeats that completely: git restores every
# one of them on each pull, so the server re-materialises the whole archive
# (measured: 461 files, 820MB of mp4/mp3/jpg/srt) - and with the auto-pull
# running every 30 minutes that happens 48 times a day. It is also what made
# .git grow to 751MB. "No archives on the server" only holds if git does not
# carry them.
longform_out/*.mp4
longform_out/*.mp3
longform_out/*.wav
longform_out/*.jpg
longform_out/*.png
longform_out/*.srt
longform_out/*.txt
longform_out/*/
longform_out/*.json
!longform_out/posted_long.json
longform_images/
short_out/
_used_images.json
check_*.png

# Demo / comparison clips left in the repo root. cleanup_artifacts.py deletes
# them too, so tracking them is pure churn.
fa_demo_*.mp4
en_demo_*.mp4
fa_hook_*.mp4
en_hook_*.mp4
fa_voice_compare.mp4
